WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.580
Ospreys nest along the Chesapeake Bay.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.000
They plunge feet-first into the water to catch fish.

3
00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:14.280
In parts of the Bay, one fish they often bring home is the Atlantic menhaden.

4
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.900
Menhaden are small fish that eat plankton, living things, most of them tiny, that

5
00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.900
drift in the water.

6
00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:22.680
So here is the question.

7
00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.700
Why does each step up this food web have less energy to live on?

8
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:30.213
Nearly every food web starts with a producer.

9
00:00:31.213 --> 00:00:32.613
A producer makes its own food.

10
00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:37.013
Most producers capture energy from sunlight and store it in food.

11
00:00:37.953 --> 00:00:42.353
In the open Bay, the main producers are phytoplankton, tiny drifting algae.

12
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:46.793
Many zooplankton, tiny drifting animals, eat phytoplankton.

13
00:00:47.593 --> 00:00:48.813
They are first-order consumers.

14
00:00:49.967 --> 00:00:53.947
A food chain is a straight-line model of one path that energy takes.

15
00:00:54.807 --> 00:00:56.687
The arrows point from the food to the eater.

16
00:00:57.627 --> 00:01:02.127
In this chain, menhaden eat zooplankton, so here they are second-order consumers.

17
00:01:03.207 --> 00:01:05.307
Menhaden also eat phytoplankton directly.

18
00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:07.527
Ospreys eat menhaden.

19
00:01:08.287 --> 00:01:10.947
A third-order consumer eats second-order consumers.

20
00:01:10.947 --> 00:01:14.147
So the osprey is a third-order consumer here.

21
00:01:15.067 --> 00:01:16.707
A menhaden eats plankton.

22
00:01:17.507 --> 00:01:20.587
Most of that energy does not become new menhaden.

23
00:01:21.347 --> 00:01:24.447
Much of it is given off as thermal energy, which is heat.

24
00:01:25.327 --> 00:01:28.467
Some of the food passes through the fish without being digested.

25
00:01:29.327 --> 00:01:33.107
Only the rest is built into the fish's body, where an osprey can get it.

26
00:01:33.807 --> 00:01:36.547
Even then, ospreys do not catch every menhaden.

27
00:01:36.547 --> 00:01:41.707
Many die without being eaten, and their energy goes to decomposers instead.

28
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.070
Stack the energy of each level as a block.

29
00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:47.610
This stack is an energy pyramid.

30
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:53.070
As a rough rule, about one tenth of the energy in one level is available to the

31
00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:57.430
next. Real measurements vary from place to place, and many are lower.

32
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.150
Here is a model with made-up numbers.

33
00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.830
Producers have 10,000 units.

34
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.600
First-order consumers have 1,000.

35
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.370
Second-order consumers have 100.

36
00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:10.750
Third-order consumers have 10.

37
00:02:11.733 --> 00:02:13.073
So here is the answer.

38
00:02:13.973 --> 00:02:16.733
At each step, much of the energy is given off as heat.

39
00:02:17.713 --> 00:02:18.973
Energy does not disappear.

40
00:02:19.893 --> 00:02:24.453
It changes form. Only roughly one tenth is left for the next level.

41
00:02:25.293 --> 00:02:29.833
In most ecosystems, energy flows one way, in as sunlight and out as heat.

42
00:02:30.713 --> 00:02:32.873
And an energy pyramid is not a head count.
